## What was built

### `src/fairness.py` — the group audit
- `group_metrics(y_true, proba, groups, threshold)` — per-group `n`,
  `actual_default_rate`, `approval_rate`, `tpr_deny_given_default` (recall for
  catching true defaulters), `fpr_deny_given_repaid` (rate of wrongly denying
  genuinely creditworthy applicants — the more consequential fairness harm),
  `auc`, `calibration_gap`, and `disparate_impact_ratio_vs_max` (the EEOC
  four-fifths-rule ratio against the most-favoured group).
- `run_fairness_audit(threshold)` — the three tables (`sex`, `age_group`,
  `foreign_worker`), computed on the **full 1,000-row dataset** rather than
  just the 200-row hold-out, because `foreign_worker = no` is only 37 rows
  overall and 6 in the test split — too small for a stable held-out-only
  estimate. Documented as a demonstration-audit tradeoff, not a methodological
  ideal, in the module docstring and the report.
- `cost_optimal_threshold()` — sweeps thresholds on the **200-row hold-out**
  against German Credit's documented cost matrix (missed bad risk = 5x a
  wrongly denied good risk).
- `train_unaware_variant()` / `compare_aware_vs_unaware()` — a second XGBoost
  model trained on the identical split minus `personal_status_sex` and
  `age_years`, compared on test-set AUC and full-dataset fairness gaps.
- `build_preprocessor()` (in `src/preprocess.py`) was generalised to accept
  custom `numeric_features`/`categorical_features` lists (default unchanged)
  so the unaware variant reuses the exact same preprocessing logic as Phase 1.

### `src/adverse_action.py`
`generate_notice(applicant, threshold=...)` — reuses Phase 2's `reason_codes()`
(now threshold-aware, see below) to produce an ECOA/Regulation-B-style
statement of reasons, clearly labelled as a capstone demonstration, not a real
institution's notice.

### Consistency fix carried into Phases 2-3
While building the notice generator I found that `explain_local()` and
`reason_codes()` used the hardcoded config `DECISION_THRESHOLD`, ignoring
whatever threshold the user set on the Streamlit app's Score page — so
"Why this decision" (page 2) could disagree with the decision shown on page 1
if the user had moved the slider. Both functions now take an explicit
`threshold` parameter (default unchanged, so Phase 2's CLI behaviour and
existing tests are unaffected); page 2 and the new adverse-action expander
pass `st.session_state.threshold` through.

